messages:
sophie_cs: Customer reports sudden 401 invalid_api_key from their SPA in prod. They say `curl` to the same endpoint with the same key succeeds. They pasted fetch snippet and network behavior.

alex_support: Can you paste the fetch snippet + what they see in devtools (request headers / response)? Also confirm they're not shipping the key in the public bundle if this is a browser app.

sophie_cs: Customer's fetch example:
```js
fetch('https://api.redwood.example.com/v1/generate', {
  method: 'POST',
  headers: {
    'Authorization': 'Bearer SK_live_XXXXXXXX',
    'Content-Type': 'application/json'
  },
  body: JSON.stringify({ prompt: 'hi' })
})
```
They say the authorization header is present in their request when testing locally, but in prod the final request to our origin doesn't show the header and they get 401. Curl with same key -> 200.

jin_eng: Two likely things: (1) The browser preflight (OPTIONS) is failing or not returning Access-Control-Allow-Headers including Authorization, so the browser strips the header. (2) A proxy/WAF in front of their app might be removing or rejecting Authorization on OPTIONS. Both would explain curl working but browser failing.

alex_support: Right — when fetch triggers a preflight, the browser first sends OPTIONS; if that response doesn't allow `Authorization` in `Access-Control-Allow-Headers`, the browser won't include it in the subsequent POST. Can we see the OPTIONS response headers from their prod origin?

sophie_cs: Customer checked and says their CDN returns 403 for the OPTIONS preflight (they suspect a WAF rule), logs: "Blocked request: disallowed method OPTIONS". They also attached a HAR showing only an OPTIONS -> 403 before any POST is attempted.

jin_eng: That matches. The 403 on OPTIONS means the browser never sends the Authorization header to our endpoint, so our gateway sees an unauthenticated request and returns 401. This is a client-side / infra misconfiguration, not a Redwood key issue.

alex_support: Suggested steps to customer (short):
- Do NOT embed long-lived API keys in frontend. Use a backend proxy or short-lived session tokens.
- Fix their CORS/WAF so OPTIONS returns 200/204 and includes these headers:
  - Access-Control-Allow-Origin: https://app.example.com
  - Access-Control-Allow-Headers: Authorization, Content-Type
  - Access-Control-Allow-Methods: POST, OPTIONS
- If they must test from browser, confirm in devtools: OPTIONS -> 204 and subsequent POST includes Authorization header.

jin_eng: Example express proxy (safe pattern):
```js
// backend (server-side)
app.post('/api/gen', async (req, res) => {
  const r = await fetch('https://api.redwood.example.com/v1/generate', {
    method: 'POST',
    headers: { 'Authorization': `Bearer ${process.env.REDWOOD_KEY}`, 'Content-Type': 'application/json' },
    body: JSON.stringify(req.body)
  });
  const data = await r.text();
  res.status(r.status).send(data);
});
```
alex_support: Also reminded them to rotate any key that may have been exposed if they were testing in-browser earlier.

sophie_cs: I'll push these notes to the customer and ask them to confirm once their WAF allows OPTIONS or after they move calls to the backend. Anything else?

jin_eng: If they want us to verify, have them paste an anonymized HAR (remove keys) showing OPTIONS/POST. We can confirm whether Authorization ever reaches our edge.

sophie_cs: will do, closing on our side once they confirm. Thanks.
